00:10:16.448 --> 00:10:19.151 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think where the improvement
that you are mentioning

00:10:19.151 --> 00:10:22.721
is coming from is the
response to the lunacy.

00:10:22.721 --> 00:10:26.058
And so you are right that
a greater number of voices

00:10:26.058 --> 00:10:29.729
in all sorts of settings are
starting to find their spines,

00:10:29.729 --> 00:10:31.930
find their testicular fortitude.

00:10:31.930 --> 00:10:34.867
So that, you know,
makes you feel good,

00:10:34.867 --> 00:10:36.702
but it moves so slowly, right?

00:10:36.702 --> 00:10:38.237
It has taken, you know,

00:10:38.237 --> 00:10:39.972 align:start size:94% position:6%
many of the people that
you've been talking about,

00:10:39.972 --> 00:10:42.141
you know, a decade, two decades.

00:10:42.141 --> 00:10:45.111
I mean, especially since I'm
in the academic ecosystem,

00:10:45.111 --> 00:10:48.247
I've been warning about
these parasitic ideas

00:10:48.247 --> 00:10:52.284
for several decades,
let alone 2014 and '15.

00:10:52.284 --> 00:10:55.855
Remember that my area
of scientific inquiry

00:10:55.855 --> 00:10:58.924 align:start position:0%
is applying evolutionary biology
and evolutionary psychology

00:10:58.924 --> 00:11:00.826
in understanding human behavior,

00:11:00.826 --> 00:11:02.628 align:start size:84% position:16%
and so a lot of these
parasitic ideas,

00:11:02.628 --> 00:11:06.598
the rejection of biology, the
rejection of sex differences

00:11:06.598 --> 00:11:10.535
existed within my
scientific pursuits

00:11:10.535 --> 00:11:12.905 align:start size:88% position:13%
before they had entered
the culture wars.

00:11:12.905 --> 00:11:15.207 align:start size:81% position:19%
And so, yes, there
is improvement,

00:11:15.207 --> 00:11:17.376 align:start size:84% position:16%
but there's a lot
more work to be done.

00:11:17.376 --> 00:11:18.477
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:11:18.477 --> 00:11:20.045
All right, now to the book,

00:11:20.045 --> 00:11:22.381
because happiness
is a big topic,

00:11:22.381 --> 00:11:24.850
and I was thinking as I was
flipping through it before,

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:26.052
there's something interesting

00:11:26.052 --> 00:11:27.652 align:start size:84% position:16%
about writing a book
about happiness,

00:11:27.652 --> 00:11:30.255
'cause it puts a lot of
pressure on the author,

00:11:30.255 --> 00:11:32.391 align:start size:88% position:13%
sort of the way I
always felt that Jordan

00:11:32.391 --> 00:11:35.294
was under a lot of pressure by
writing "12 Rules for Life."

00:11:35.294 --> 00:11:38.063 align:start size:88% position:13%
Because man, if he broke
one of those rules,

00:11:38.063 --> 00:11:40.032
then why would you
wanna buy this book?

00:11:40.032 --> 00:11:42.368
And to Jordan's credit,
people always ask me,

00:11:42.368 --> 00:11:43.769
I never saw him break a rule.

00:11:43.769 --> 00:11:45.137
I really, in all the
years I've known him,

00:11:45.137 --> 00:11:46.672
I've never seen him do it.

00:11:46.672 --> 00:11:48.641 align:start size:81% position:19%
But at a happiness
level, to say,

00:11:48.641 --> 00:11:50.176
"I'm going to be the guy

00:11:50.176 --> 00:11:53.179 align:start size:88% position:13%
that's gonna tell
people how to be happy,"

00:11:53.179 --> 00:11:54.346
it's a lot of pressure.

00:11:54.346 --> 00:11:56.148
Now, I know you very well

00:11:56.148 --> 00:11:57.650
and you're happy and smiley

00:11:57.650 --> 00:12:00.052
and you love your wife and
your kids and all that stuff,

00:12:00.052 --> 00:12:03.889
but how much work in a life
that's been fairly complex

00:12:03.889 --> 00:12:05.791
and had its share of
tragedy, actually,

00:12:05.791 --> 00:12:08.160
was it to get to that
place and then to say,

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:11.163
"Oh, I can be a expert on this"?

00:12:11.163 --> 00:12:12.264
- Well, thank you.

00:12:12.264 --> 00:12:13.432
That's a fantastic question.

00:12:13.432 --> 00:12:15.267 align:start size:84% position:16%
So maybe I'll
start by explaining

00:12:15.267 --> 00:12:18.237
how I came to write
a book on happiness.

00:12:18.237 --> 00:12:20.205 align:start size:84% position:16%
If you would've asked
me five years ago

00:12:20.205 --> 00:12:22.741 align:start size:94% position:6%
was that within my radar,
I would've said no.

00:12:22.741 --> 00:12:23.776
What led me to write,

00:12:23.776 --> 00:12:24.910
and I actually discussed this

00:12:24.910 --> 00:12:26.378 align:start size:84% position:16%
in the first
chapter of the book,

00:12:26.378 --> 00:12:29.982
is that I noticed that
in my social engagements,

00:12:29.982 --> 00:12:33.319
some of the most
powerful responses

00:12:33.319 --> 00:12:35.020
that I would receive from people

00:12:35.020 --> 00:12:38.290 align:start size:97% position:3%
is when I offered some
prescriptive advice, right?

00:12:38.290 --> 00:12:41.193
Typically, I operate as
a behavioral scientist.

00:12:41.193 --> 00:12:42.828
I operate in descriptive world.

00:12:42.828 --> 00:12:45.931
I just describe why do
people do what they do

00:12:45.931 --> 00:12:48.067
without saying, "This
is what you should do."

00:12:48.067 --> 00:12:50.736
That was very much
more the purview of,

00:12:50.736 --> 00:12:52.271
as you mentioned,
Jordan Peterson.

00:12:52.271 --> 00:12:54.373
And also, especially because
he's a clinical psychologist,

00:12:54.373 --> 00:12:55.875
so he's helping people.

00:12:55.875 --> 00:12:59.178 align:start size:97% position:3%
"Don't do this pattern,
follow this pattern," right?

00:12:59.178 --> 00:13:00.979 align:start size:88% position:13%
But then when I
noticed how much people

00:13:00.979 --> 00:13:04.550 align:start size:88% position:13%
would respond
favorably to, you know,

00:13:04.550 --> 00:13:07.786 align:start size:84% position:16%
some, you know,
100 character thing

00:13:07.786 --> 00:13:09.321
that I put up on Twitter,

00:13:09.321 --> 00:13:10.556
and then they're saying,

00:13:10.556 --> 00:13:11.490
"My God, that's
changed my life."

00:13:11.490 --> 00:13:12.957
And I would say, "Really?

00:13:12.957 --> 00:13:16.529 align:start size:97% position:3%
That struck me as a rather
banal, obvious point to make."

00:13:16.529 --> 00:13:18.497 align:start size:88% position:13%
And then so people would
write to me and say,

00:13:18.497 --> 00:13:21.100
"Even though you're dealing
with such serious issues,

00:13:21.100 --> 00:13:22.834
you always seem to be playful

00:13:22.834 --> 00:13:23.935
and there's always a
twinkle in your eye

00:13:23.935 --> 00:13:25.371
and you're always happy.

00:13:25.371 --> 00:13:27.238
What's the secret, professor?"

00:13:27.238 --> 00:13:29.675 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so I said, "You know
what? Why don't I take a..."

00:13:29.675 --> 00:13:31.577 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because "Parasitic
Mind," the previous book,

00:13:31.577 --> 00:13:34.046
was about negative mindsets,

00:13:34.046 --> 00:13:37.148
parasitic ideas that
infect your brain.

00:13:37.148 --> 00:13:39.785
I thought, "Well, why don't
I now complete the story

00:13:39.785 --> 00:13:42.955 align:start size:88% position:13%
and talk about, you
know, winning mindsets,

00:13:42.955 --> 00:13:44.223 align:start size:84% position:16%
optimal choices
that you can make."

00:13:44.223 --> 00:13:46.591
But to your point, though,

00:13:46.591 --> 00:13:49.194
I had the epistemic
humility, hopefully,

00:13:49.194 --> 00:13:52.064
in writing this book,
not to guarantee you,

00:13:52.064 --> 00:13:55.234
"Oh, if you implement these,
I guarantee you'll be happy."

00:13:55.234 --> 00:13:58.938
All I can say is that
statistically speaking,

00:13:58.938 --> 00:14:02.307 align:start size:97% position:3%
if you were to implement
these or adopt these mindsets,

00:14:02.307 --> 00:14:05.777 align:start size:97% position:3%
it increases the probability
of you being happy.

00:14:05.777 --> 00:14:07.045 align:start size:81% position:19%
So I can't-
- Let me ask you.

00:14:07.045 --> 00:14:08.280
- Yeah.

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:09.882
- Well, as a man of
science, as you know,

00:14:09.882 --> 00:14:11.483
I have two young boys here,

00:14:11.483 --> 00:14:13.118
and they're only
two months apart.

00:14:13.118 --> 00:14:15.220
But Justin, who's my oldest,

00:14:15.220 --> 00:14:18.490
this kid came out
into this world

00:14:18.490 --> 00:14:21.994 align:start size:88% position:13%
with a smile on his face
and a zest for life,

00:14:21.994 --> 00:14:24.196
and, "I'm gonna get everything."

00:14:24.196 --> 00:14:26.699
And Luke came out much more,

00:14:27.933 --> 00:14:29.767
"I'm gonna look you up and down,

00:14:29.767 --> 00:14:33.172 align:start position:0%
I'm gonna figure out what's
going on here, think about it."

00:14:33.172 --> 00:14:35.441
And to me so much of that,

00:14:35.441 --> 00:14:38.110
now spending so much
time with them as I do,

00:14:38.110 --> 00:14:41.347
seems hardwired, the either,

00:14:41.347 --> 00:14:42.481 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't wanna say
desire for happiness,

00:14:42.481 --> 00:14:44.950 align:start size:88% position:13%
but the predetermination
or something

00:14:44.950 --> 00:14:47.319
that would lead someone to
be just smiling all the time

00:14:47.319 --> 00:14:50.489 align:start size:88% position:13%
versus someone that runs
a little bit cooler.

00:14:50.489 --> 00:14:53.492 align:start size:84% position:16%
How do you factor
that into, you know,

00:14:53.492 --> 00:14:55.193 align:start size:97% position:3%
what makes someone happy?
- Again, fantastic question.

00:14:55.193 --> 00:14:59.964 align:start size:88% position:13%
About 50% of differences
across people

00:14:59.964 --> 00:15:03.935 align:start size:97% position:3%
in terms of their
happiness stems from genes.

00:15:03.935 --> 00:15:06.104 align:start size:84% position:16%
So at first glance,
you might say,

00:15:06.104 --> 00:15:08.006 align:start size:84% position:16%
"Oh, so it's hardwired
in my genes?"

00:15:08.006 --> 00:15:12.077
No, because that means
there's 50% up for grabs.

00:15:12.077 --> 00:15:16.181
So Justin is born with
the sunnier disposition,

00:15:16.181 --> 00:15:19.584 align:start size:88% position:13%
so in a sense, climbing
Mount Happiness,

00:15:19.584 --> 00:15:22.054
he's starting off ahead.

00:15:22.054 --> 00:15:25.958
But if he then with the 50%
that's left up for grabs,

00:15:25.958 --> 00:15:29.594
if he then adopts mindsets

00:15:29.594 --> 00:15:31.963
and takes decisions
throughout his life

00:15:31.963 --> 00:15:34.633 align:start size:97% position:3%
that don't optimize
happiness, it's very likely...

00:15:34.633 --> 00:15:36.201
Is Luke the second one?

00:15:36.201 --> 00:15:37.569
- Yep.
- Luke.

00:15:37.569 --> 00:15:41.073
It's very well possible
that Luke can, you know,

00:15:41.073 --> 00:15:42.341
overshoot him in happiness.

00:15:42.341 --> 00:15:44.576
So I say that right off the bat.

00:15:44.576 --> 00:15:48.580
It's obviously clear that some
of us wake up in the morning

00:15:48.580 --> 00:15:52.251 align:start size:97% position:3%
with a completely
different daily disposition

00:15:52.251 --> 00:15:54.553 align:start size:84% position:16%
than someone who's
more sullen and so on.

00:15:54.553 --> 00:15:56.188
But again, that's good news.

00:15:56.188 --> 00:15:59.325
50% is up for you to change,

00:15:59.325 --> 00:16:01.626 align:start size:88% position:13%
and so it's exactly
what you're saying, yes.

00:16:01.626 --> 00:16:03.161
- It's funny because
even watching them,

00:16:03.161 --> 00:16:05.597
it took Luke a little bit
longer to start crawling,

00:16:05.597 --> 00:16:07.198
and then the second
he started crawling,

00:16:07.198 --> 00:16:08.901 align:start size:84% position:16%
I noticed he started
smiling more.

00:16:08.901 --> 00:16:11.136
And it was almost as
if he wasn't that happy

00:16:11.136 --> 00:16:13.038
'cause he was kind of stuck,

00:16:13.038 --> 00:16:14.840
and then once he started moving,

00:16:14.840 --> 00:16:16.641
then he allowed a little
happiness to get in there.

00:16:16.641 --> 00:16:17.909
But let's talk about,

00:16:17.909 --> 00:16:19.378
since we've mentioned
Jordan a couple times,

00:16:19.378 --> 00:16:20.879
one of the things that
he talks a lot about

00:16:20.879 --> 00:16:23.615 align:start size:84% position:16%
that I think has
helped a lot of people

00:16:23.615 --> 00:16:25.884 align:start size:88% position:13%
is that your goal
shouldn't be happiness.

00:16:25.884 --> 00:16:28.153
Your goal should be purpose.

00:16:28.153 --> 00:16:30.989
First off, I sense
you agree with that,

00:16:30.989 --> 00:16:34.126
but can you explain how
do you meld those things

00:16:34.126 --> 00:16:35.694
and what do you see as the
difference of those things?

00:16:35.694 --> 00:16:38.930
- Yeah, so in the last chapter

00:16:38.930 --> 00:16:41.200
of my forthcoming
happiness book,

00:16:41.200 --> 00:16:44.236
I have a quote by Viktor Frankl,

00:16:44.236 --> 00:16:45.704 align:start size:81% position:19%
where he basically
says, you know,

00:16:45.704 --> 00:16:49.074
"Don't pursue directly success.

00:16:49.074 --> 00:16:51.410 align:start size:88% position:13%
It is something that
comes as a consequence."

00:16:51.410 --> 00:16:53.111
And I use that because I argue

00:16:53.111 --> 00:16:56.415 align:start size:97% position:3%
that I completely analogize
that to happiness,

00:16:56.415 --> 00:16:57.749
and hence, I agree with Jordan.

00:16:57.749 --> 00:17:01.687 align:start size:84% position:16%
Happiness should be
something that results

00:17:01.687 --> 00:17:05.223 align:start size:88% position:13%
from you having adopted
the right mindsets,

00:17:05.223 --> 00:17:06.625
the right decisions,

00:17:06.625 --> 00:17:09.061 align:start size:97% position:3%
and so I think you mentioned
purpose and meaning.

00:17:09.061 --> 00:17:10.495
So in one of the chapters

00:17:10.495 --> 00:17:12.731
where I talk about
the two decisions

00:17:12.731 --> 00:17:15.667
that are most likely to impart
either great amount of misery

00:17:15.667 --> 00:17:17.969
or great amount of
happiness to you,

00:17:17.969 --> 00:17:20.906
I say that the choice of
spouse that you choose

00:17:20.906 --> 00:17:24.510 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the choice of
profession that you choose

00:17:24.510 --> 00:17:26.311 align:start size:84% position:16%
are the ones that
are going to do that,

00:17:26.311 --> 00:17:29.081
either make you more
miserable or happier.

00:17:29.081 --> 00:17:32.384 align:start size:84% position:16%
And to Jordan's
point about purpose,

00:17:33.718 --> 00:17:36.822 align:start size:97% position:3%
I wake up every day, you
know, with sort of a gleeful,

00:17:36.822 --> 00:17:39.358 align:start size:88% position:13%
I'm rubbing my hands
together in anticipation

00:17:39.358 --> 00:17:43.928
because my job brings me
great purpose and meaning.

00:17:43.928 --> 00:17:45.931 align:start size:84% position:16%
Today, I'm gonna
talk with Dave Rubin.

00:17:45.931 --> 00:17:47.532
Later, I'm gonna meet
a graduate student.

00:17:47.532 --> 00:17:49.668 align:start size:88% position:13%
Then, I'm gonna speak
to a college in Florida.

00:17:49.668 --> 00:17:52.004
Then, I'm gonna put
up a Saad Truth.

00:17:52.004 --> 00:17:54.272
I'm constantly in creative mode,

00:17:54.272 --> 00:17:55.940 align:start size:97% position:3%
so that's actually something
I talk about in the book,

00:17:55.940 --> 00:18:00.345
that the surest way to
find purpose and meaning,

00:18:00.345 --> 00:18:02.781
irrespective of which
profession you follow,

00:18:02.781 --> 00:18:05.350 align:start size:84% position:16%
is to instantiate
your creative impulse.

00:18:05.350 --> 00:18:06.785
Now, that's a very
broad statement,

00:18:06.785 --> 00:18:09.487
because I could be a
chef and be creative.

00:18:09.487 --> 00:18:10.988
I could be an architect,

00:18:10.988 --> 00:18:14.225 align:start size:94% position:6%
I could be a standup comic
creating new material.

00:18:14.225 --> 00:18:15.527
I could be a podcaster.

00:18:15.527 --> 00:18:17.129 align:start size:84% position:16%
I could be an author
and a professor.

00:18:17.129 --> 00:18:19.397
But all other things equal,

00:18:19.397 --> 00:18:23.000
if you immerse yourself
in the creative impulse,

00:18:23.000 --> 00:18:24.803 align:start size:94% position:6%
you're more likely to
find purpose and meaning.

00:18:24.803 --> 00:18:29.241 align:start size:97% position:3%
Very few insurance adjusters
probably wake up and say,

00:18:29.241 --> 00:18:32.144
"Thank God I'm an
insurance adjuster.

00:18:32.144 --> 00:18:34.046
I find such purpose and
meaning doing that."

00:18:34.046 --> 00:18:35.680
- So what do you
do for that person?

00:18:35.680 --> 00:18:37.482 align:start size:88% position:13%
So for the person that's
an insurance adjuster

00:18:37.482 --> 00:18:38.750
that's watching this right now,

00:18:38.750 --> 00:18:42.220
that it's a mundane
activity, let's say.

00:18:42.220 --> 00:18:43.722
They might have
the great spouse,

00:18:43.722 --> 00:18:45.557 align:start size:84% position:16%
they might be making
a decent living,

00:18:45.557 --> 00:18:46.925
but the day-to-day thing,

00:18:46.925 --> 00:18:48.293
and I'm sure there are
some insurance adjusters

00:18:48.293 --> 00:18:49.761
who absolutely freaking love it.

00:18:49.761 --> 00:18:51.896
But whatever that job is,

00:18:51.896 --> 00:18:53.398 align:start size:84% position:16%
whatever we're
talking about there,

00:18:53.398 --> 00:18:55.533
what do you say to that person?

00:18:55.533 --> 00:18:56.701
- Yeah, fantastic.

00:18:56.701 --> 00:18:57.502 align:start size:84% position:16%
So there, I would say-
- Who's pulling

00:18:57.502 --> 00:18:58.403
the levers all day.

00:18:58.403 --> 00:18:59.871
- That's right.

00:18:59.871 --> 00:19:03.341
So in a sense, it relates to
some of the other prescriptions

00:19:03.341 --> 00:19:04.175
that I have in the book.

00:19:04.175 --> 00:19:05.944
So I argue, for example,

00:19:05.944 --> 00:19:10.048
I have a whole chapter titled
"Life as a Playground," right?

00:19:10.048 --> 00:19:13.351 align:start size:94% position:6%
Meaning that immerse
yourself in endless play,

00:19:13.351 --> 00:19:15.554
even when pursuing
very serious things.

00:19:15.554 --> 00:19:17.556 align:start size:84% position:16%
So science, the
pursuit of science,

00:19:17.556 --> 00:19:20.759
is the ultimate form of
intellectual play, right?

00:19:20.759 --> 00:19:22.126
Because when you're
putting together,

00:19:22.126 --> 00:19:23.995 align:start position:0%
I'm gonna come to the
insurance adjuster in a second,

00:19:23.995 --> 00:19:27.832
when you're putting together a
puzzle, a 1,000 piece puzzle,

00:19:27.832 --> 00:19:30.201
you're trying to make
all these pieces fit.

00:19:30.201 --> 00:19:31.703
Well, that's what
science is, right?

00:19:31.703 --> 00:19:33.438
There's a bunch of
variables out there,

00:19:33.438 --> 00:19:36.207
and I'm trying to find which
one causes which other one,

00:19:36.207 --> 00:19:37.643 align:start size:84% position:16%
which one correlates
to the other,

00:19:37.643 --> 00:19:38.810
or is unrelated to the other.

00:19:38.810 --> 00:19:42.915 align:start size:88% position:13%
So it's a form of
orgiastic cerebral play.

00:19:42.915 --> 00:19:45.784
So to the insurance adjuster,

00:19:45.784 --> 00:19:49.087
I say that perhaps your job

00:19:49.087 --> 00:19:52.457
doesn't allow you that
creative play pursuit,

00:19:52.457 --> 00:19:55.694
but then at night, when
you finish your job,

00:19:55.694 --> 00:19:59.798 align:start position:0%
rather than watching television
for the next five hours,

00:19:59.798 --> 00:20:02.534
maybe sign up for
that ceramics class

00:20:02.534 --> 00:20:04.136 align:start size:84% position:16%
that you had always
wanted to take.

