Meaning that men relax their
threshold of acceptability

00:30:13.444 --> 00:30:16.981
as the night of loneliness
looms in the background.

00:30:16.981 --> 00:30:19.417
But you don't see that the
other way around, right?

00:30:19.417 --> 00:30:21.452
There is no song that's
been written called,

00:30:21.452 --> 00:30:24.422
"Don't the Guys Get More
Handsome Near Closing Time"

00:30:24.422 --> 00:30:28.726
because the though of making
a wrong choice loom as large,

00:30:28.726 --> 00:30:31.963
whether it's at 10
in the afternoon

00:30:31.963 --> 00:30:33.364 align:start size:81% position:19%
or three in the
morning for women.

00:30:33.364 --> 00:30:36.434
And so I discuss all of
these things, actually,

00:30:36.434 --> 00:30:39.470
in the happiness book when
it comes to mate search.

00:30:39.470 --> 00:30:43.541 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Are you surprised how
many people are not happy?

00:30:43.541 --> 00:30:46.044
I mean, you know, we've
been blessed to travel

00:30:46.044 --> 00:30:47.579
in some pretty great circles,

00:30:47.579 --> 00:30:48.880 align:start size:84% position:16%
and, you know, I
know plenty of people

00:30:48.880 --> 00:30:50.582
that I think are at
least roughly happy

00:30:50.582 --> 00:30:52.383
or trying to do
something decent,

00:30:52.383 --> 00:30:54.786 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I know a decent
amount of miserable people

00:30:54.786 --> 00:30:57.188 align:start size:97% position:3%
that have an awful lot in
terms of what you would think

00:30:57.188 --> 00:30:59.991 align:start size:84% position:16%
are the pieces that
make you happy.

00:30:59.991 --> 00:31:01.558
- Oh, you are absolutely right.

00:31:01.558 --> 00:31:03.994
I mean, we see it
on Twitter, right?

00:31:03.994 --> 00:31:07.999 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, you know, I was away
for 16 days in Portugal,

00:31:07.999 --> 00:31:11.569
and so I only posted,
you know, beach photos.

00:31:11.569 --> 00:31:13.070
I didn't weigh in on anything.

00:31:13.070 --> 00:31:16.540
So I was still posting things,
but never checking anything.

00:31:16.540 --> 00:31:20.611 align:start position:0%
And I didn't check what my
blood pressure was pre and post,

00:31:20.611 --> 00:31:24.882 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I'm almost certain
there was a huge improvement

00:31:24.882 --> 00:31:26.484
because I wasn't exposed

00:31:26.484 --> 00:31:29.187 align:start size:84% position:16%
to the endless misery
of people, right?

00:31:29.187 --> 00:31:31.021
And you know me well, Dave.

00:31:31.021 --> 00:31:33.124 align:start size:84% position:16%
Even when I'm going
after someone,

00:31:33.124 --> 00:31:37.395
even when I am, you
know, insulting them
in some spicy way,

00:31:37.395 --> 00:31:40.030
I always do it, I hope so,

00:31:40.030 --> 00:31:42.233
you know, with a
playful mindset.

00:31:42.233 --> 00:31:44.269
So it does actually amaze me

00:31:44.269 --> 00:31:45.503
that people can be so miserable.

00:31:45.503 --> 00:31:48.873
Now, I wonder, and
here I'm speculating,

00:31:48.873 --> 00:31:51.709
but I think there is value
in what I'm saying here,

00:31:51.709 --> 00:31:56.681
I wonder if the fact that
I had my childhood history,

00:31:57.448 --> 00:31:58.683
I mean, you know it,

00:31:58.683 --> 00:31:59.984 align:start size:84% position:16%
but maybe some of
your listeners, right?

00:31:59.984 --> 00:32:03.554
I mean, I'm a product of
the Lebanese Civil War.

00:32:03.554 --> 00:32:06.991
So not withstanding that I
have a sunny disposition,

00:32:06.991 --> 00:32:08.826
the fact that I've seen

00:32:08.826 --> 00:32:12.563 align:start size:88% position:13%
some of the truly
horrible things of life,

00:32:12.563 --> 00:32:16.167
maybe that even adds to
my ability to be happy.

00:32:16.167 --> 00:32:19.270 align:start size:81% position:19%
Because every time
I find a reason

00:32:19.270 --> 00:32:20.771
to be pissed off at something,

00:32:20.771 --> 00:32:24.742
I can easily insert that
voice in my head that says,

00:32:24.742 --> 00:32:29.047 align:start size:94% position:6%
"You could have not gotten
out of Lebanon in 1975,

00:32:29.047 --> 00:32:30.748
so why don't you
stop your whining,"

00:32:30.748 --> 00:32:32.249
this is my internal voice,

00:32:32.249 --> 00:32:33.918
"and just go out there
and appreciate life?"

00:32:33.918 --> 00:32:37.455
So I think in some
interesting way,

00:32:37.455 --> 00:32:39.624
the fact that I've had to face

00:32:39.624 --> 00:32:42.960 align:start size:94% position:6%
these anti-fragile
stressors in my childhood

00:32:42.960 --> 00:32:46.664
allows me to be that much
happier in my day-to-day.

00:32:46.664 --> 00:32:49.200 align:start size:88% position:13%
And maybe those Twitter
miserable people,

00:32:49.200 --> 00:32:51.836
they need to go to Raqqa, Syria

00:32:51.836 --> 00:32:53.404
and see how people live there,

00:32:53.404 --> 00:32:55.940
and then maybe they won't
be such miserable cretins.

00:32:55.940 --> 00:32:57.275
- What advice would
you give for people

00:32:57.275 --> 00:32:59.110
on the social media
front in that regard?

00:32:59.110 --> 00:33:00.844 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, I do my
August off the grid,

00:33:00.844 --> 00:33:03.247 align:start size:94% position:6%
which this will be my
seventh year of doing it.

00:33:03.247 --> 00:33:06.184
I think it actually
is one of the things

00:33:06.184 --> 00:33:07.451
that has kept me happy and sane

00:33:07.451 --> 00:33:09.420 align:start size:88% position:13%
and clear-minded
throughout all of this.

00:33:09.420 --> 00:33:11.355
And I didn't lose my
mind during COVID,

00:33:11.355 --> 00:33:14.692 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I didn't lose my mind
during Trump or any of those.

00:33:14.692 --> 00:33:16.193
Well, I guess that's for
other people to judge,

00:33:16.193 --> 00:33:18.896
but ballpark, I think I
kind of made it out okay.

00:33:18.896 --> 00:33:20.698
But that social media,

00:33:20.698 --> 00:33:22.533 align:start size:88% position:13%
which of course the
word social's in there,

00:33:22.533 --> 00:33:23.468
we're social creatures.

00:33:23.468 --> 00:33:25.569
It's designed to make us social,

00:33:25.569 --> 00:33:29.207
but it actually seemingly
made us more antisocial,

00:33:29.207 --> 00:33:32.209 align:start size:84% position:16%
more neurotic and
fearful of strangers,

00:33:32.209 --> 00:33:33.978
and all sorts of stuff.

00:33:35.379 --> 00:33:39.917
- Well, maybe I could answer
this by drawing an analogy.

00:33:39.917 --> 00:33:42.219
You know, we are a
storytelling animal,

00:33:42.219 --> 00:33:43.988
so we often learn best

00:33:43.988 --> 00:33:47.891 align:start size:88% position:13%
not by having sort of
academic abstract ideas,

00:33:47.891 --> 00:33:50.061 align:start size:84% position:16%
but by contextualizing
it with a story.

00:33:50.061 --> 00:33:54.665
So to those social media,
you know, sullen folks,

00:33:54.665 --> 00:33:56.267
listen to these two stories.

00:33:56.267 --> 00:34:00.605
So in the last chapter of "The
Saad Truth about Happiness,"

00:34:00.605 --> 00:34:03.641
I discuss two powerful stories

00:34:03.641 --> 00:34:06.343
that capture the mindset

00:34:06.343 --> 00:34:09.680 align:start size:97% position:3%
that is the antithesis of
those people on social media.

00:34:09.680 --> 00:34:11.148
So, two stories.

00:34:11.148 --> 00:34:15.152 align:start size:97% position:3%
Number one, arguably the
most incredible conversation

00:34:15.152 --> 00:34:16.487
I've had on my show,

00:34:16.487 --> 00:34:18.523
and that's saying a lot
because just like you,

00:34:18.523 --> 00:34:20.057 align:start size:84% position:16%
I've been fortunate
enough to speak

00:34:20.057 --> 00:34:22.226 align:start size:81% position:19%
to all sorts of
incredible people,

00:34:22.226 --> 00:34:24.628
is a gentleman by the
name of David McCallum,

00:34:24.628 --> 00:34:26.831 align:start size:88% position:13%
whom you probably
don't know who that is.

00:34:26.831 --> 00:34:31.903 align:start size:97% position:3%
David McCallum is someone
who spent 29 years in prison

00:34:33.004 --> 00:34:34.539
for a murder that he was
eventually exonerated.

00:34:34.539 --> 00:34:38.876
I think he went into
prison at 17 years old,

00:34:38.876 --> 00:34:41.345 align:start size:84% position:16%
and he came out well
into his forties.

00:34:41.345 --> 00:34:45.082 align:start size:84% position:16%
And as we were
chatting on the show,

00:34:45.082 --> 00:34:47.418
I looked at him, and you
can go watch our chat,

00:34:47.418 --> 00:34:48.953
it's really quite powerful.

00:34:48.953 --> 00:34:52.189 align:start size:88% position:13%
I said to him, "You
know, I'm amazed, David.

00:34:52.189 --> 00:34:54.892
I mean, you must be a
reincarnation of Buddha

00:34:54.892 --> 00:34:59.463
because I'm amazed that you're
not filled with vengefulness,

00:34:59.463 --> 00:35:01.732
with vindictiveness, with anger.

00:35:01.732 --> 00:35:03.501 align:start size:84% position:16%
You're a much better
man than I am,

00:35:03.501 --> 00:35:06.137 align:start size:84% position:16%
because if I had been
in your position,

00:35:06.137 --> 00:35:09.406
and if 30, 29 years had
been stolen from me,

00:35:09.406 --> 00:35:12.042
I would wanna burn
the world down."

00:35:12.042 --> 00:35:13.310
And then his answer,

00:35:13.310 --> 00:35:15.513
so to speak to those
social media people

00:35:15.513 --> 00:35:17.582
who, you know, go crazy
about every little thing,

00:35:17.582 --> 00:35:20.851 align:start size:84% position:16%
he said, "You know,
I have a sister

00:35:20.851 --> 00:35:23.620
who's been bedridden
with cerebral palsy

00:35:23.620 --> 00:35:25.856
for much of her life,

00:35:25.856 --> 00:35:28.659
and yet she still
finds time to smile

00:35:28.659 --> 00:35:30.694
and be grateful
for what she has.

00:35:30.694 --> 00:35:33.397
And so viewed from
that perspective,

00:35:33.397 --> 00:35:35.299 align:start size:88% position:13%
I don't really have
much to be angry about."

00:35:35.299 --> 00:35:37.869 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, that's an
incredibly powerful story.

00:35:37.869 --> 00:35:39.437 align:start size:84% position:16%
Second guy that
I'll tell you about

00:35:39.437 --> 00:35:42.273
is a gentleman whom I met.

00:35:42.273 --> 00:35:43.908
So when I was a professor

00:35:43.908 --> 00:35:46.010
at University of
California Irvine,

00:35:46.010 --> 00:35:48.779 align:start size:84% position:16%
I was sitting at a
cafe in Newport Beach,

00:35:48.779 --> 00:35:51.281
you know, working on
some paper, whatever,

00:35:51.281 --> 00:35:54.351
and I had a whole bunch of
books all over the table.

00:35:54.351 --> 00:35:55.819 align:start size:81% position:19%
And this gentleman
comes up, says,

00:35:55.819 --> 00:35:58.589
"Oh, lovely collection
of books you've got here.

00:35:58.589 --> 00:36:00.224
Do you mind if I sit
with you and chat?"

00:36:00.224 --> 00:36:04.995
It turned out that he was
a PhD student at UC Irvine,

00:36:04.995 --> 00:36:07.431 align:start size:84% position:16%
completing his
doctoral dissertation

00:36:07.431 --> 00:36:09.700
on studying homelessness.

00:36:09.700 --> 00:36:13.137 align:start size:97% position:3%
He had immersed himself
within the homeless community

00:36:13.137 --> 00:36:16.206 align:start size:88% position:13%
to learn more about
sort of their ecosystem,

00:36:16.206 --> 00:36:19.376 align:start size:88% position:13%
and eventually he
became homeless himself.

00:36:19.376 --> 00:36:21.878 align:start size:84% position:16%
And he was a
wealthy Persian guy.

00:36:21.878 --> 00:36:24.348
So he came from money,
he had a lot of money,

00:36:24.348 --> 00:36:27.118 align:start size:97% position:3%
and then several years later
he became a homeless guy.

00:36:27.118 --> 00:36:29.186
Well, I tracked him down.

00:36:29.186 --> 00:36:32.023
There's an article that I
cite in the book from 2011,

00:36:32.023 --> 00:36:34.425 align:start size:88% position:13%
so this is about 10
years after we had met,

00:36:34.425 --> 00:36:36.560
where someone is asking him,

00:36:36.560 --> 00:36:38.929
"Are you happy? Are you sad?

00:36:38.929 --> 00:36:41.331 align:start size:97% position:3%
How do you deal with what's
happened in your life?"

00:36:41.331 --> 00:36:43.801 align:start size:84% position:16%
And his answer, again,
is a good answer

00:36:43.801 --> 00:36:45.769 align:start size:84% position:16%
to those social
media sullen people.

00:36:45.769 --> 00:36:48.739 align:start size:81% position:19%
He said, "I've got
access to a gym

00:36:48.739 --> 00:36:51.008
where I can keep
my body healthy.

00:36:51.008 --> 00:36:54.712
I've got access to the
Newport Beach Library

00:36:54.712 --> 00:36:58.082
so I can keep my mind nourished.

00:36:58.082 --> 00:37:01.285
I've got nothing to be
angry or sad about."

00:37:01.285 --> 00:37:02.987
Those are powerful stories.

00:37:02.987 --> 00:37:06.490 align:start size:97% position:3%
So to those people who go
crazy about Trump and so on,

00:37:06.490 --> 00:37:08.859 align:start size:84% position:16%
there is such a bigger
world out there.

00:37:08.859 --> 00:37:11.062
There is such misery out there.

00:37:11.062 --> 00:37:12.563
Be grateful.

00:37:12.563 --> 00:37:14.198
- Can I tell you something
that just popped in my head

00:37:14.198 --> 00:37:15.665
as you were telling that story?

00:37:15.665 --> 00:37:17.668
I have not thought about
this for probably 20 years,

00:37:17.668 --> 00:37:19.135
I'm not kidding you,

00:37:19.135 --> 00:37:22.473 align:start size:97% position:3%
but when I was in my mid
twenties and I was messed up

00:37:22.473 --> 00:37:24.642 align:start size:84% position:16%
because I was closeted
and, you know,

00:37:24.642 --> 00:37:27.945
struggling stand up, and I had
no money, all of that stuff,

00:37:27.945 --> 00:37:29.914
I used to stand on the
corners in Times Square,

00:37:29.914 --> 00:37:32.216
handing out tickets to get
people to come to the shows.

00:37:32.216 --> 00:37:34.084
That's how you get stage time.

00:37:34.084 --> 00:37:35.819 align:start size:88% position:13%
And there was this
homeless guy out there,

00:37:35.819 --> 00:37:37.154
his name was Charlie,

00:37:37.154 --> 00:37:38.555
'cause he was always panhandling

00:37:38.555 --> 00:37:39.690 align:start size:84% position:16%
while we were
handing out tickets.

00:37:39.690 --> 00:37:40.524
And I always used to think,

00:37:40.524 --> 00:37:42.026
"It's kind of funny,

00:37:42.026 --> 00:37:43.260 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'm basically doing the same
thing as a homeless guy."

00:37:43.260 --> 00:37:45.429 align:start size:84% position:16%
But Charlie had the
biggest freaking smile

00:37:45.429 --> 00:37:46.530
on his face all the time,

00:37:46.530 --> 00:37:48.032 align:start size:84% position:16%
which I very
specifically remember

00:37:48.032 --> 00:37:50.234
'cause his teeth were horribly
mangled and everything else,

00:37:50.234 --> 00:37:52.903 align:start size:97% position:3%
but always was smiling,
joking around with everybody.

00:37:52.903 --> 00:37:56.407 align:start size:97% position:3%
He had a very funny sign about
how he was gonna, he said,

00:37:56.407 --> 00:37:57.674
"I'm not gonna bullshit you,

00:37:57.674 --> 00:37:59.877
I'm just gonna spend the
money on booze and weed,

00:37:59.877 --> 00:38:01.144
but come on, why not?"

00:38:01.144 --> 00:38:02.045
And he was just funny.

00:38:02.045 --> 00:38:03.280
And I remember thinking,

00:38:03.280 --> 00:38:05.315
"That homeless man
is happier than me."

00:38:05.315 --> 00:38:07.885
And that is one of the things
that started turning me around

00:38:07.885 --> 00:38:10.354 align:start position:0%
because I thought, "I have
every opportunity in this world.

00:38:10.354 --> 00:38:11.689
This guy has none."

00:38:11.689 --> 00:38:13.390
And I truly have not
thought about that

00:38:13.390 --> 00:38:14.725
for 20 some odd years,

00:38:14.725 --> 00:38:15.959
and that just sparked out of me.

00:38:15.959 --> 00:38:17.227 align:start size:81% position:19%
- I'm glad that
it triggered that.

00:38:17.227 --> 00:38:21.465
Look, right now I've
started my big tour,

00:38:21.465 --> 00:38:23.267
this media thing for the book,

00:38:23.267 --> 00:38:25.335
and so, you know, this weekend

00:38:25.335 --> 00:38:27.371 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was looking at my entire
schedule, I was like,

00:38:27.371 --> 00:38:30.207 align:start size:97% position:3%
"Oh my God, I've gotta travel
here, I gotta do this."

00:38:30.207 --> 00:38:31.442
And then I stopped.

00:38:31.442 --> 00:38:35.012
I said, "Am I really
whining about the fact

00:38:35.012 --> 00:38:37.381
that I've got all
these opportunities

00:38:37.381 --> 00:38:40.017
and I have a book coming out?

00:38:40.017 --> 00:38:41.619
Eff off, Gad Saad."

00:38:41.619 --> 00:38:44.322
And so I think if you've got

00:38:44.322 --> 00:38:47.057
that internal voice
of authenticity,

00:38:47.057 --> 00:38:49.393
you're always able
to snap yourself

00:38:49.393 --> 00:38:52.429
back into the right mindset.

00:38:52.429 --> 00:38:53.964
- Yeah.

00:38:53.964 --> 00:38:55.966
What are some of the other
tricks that people can use

00:38:55.966 --> 00:38:57.167
if they're really in a funk?

00:38:57.167 --> 00:38:58.369
You know, when somebody,

00:38:58.369 --> 00:38:59.303 align:start size:84% position:16%
when you're in those
moments in life

00:38:59.303 --> 00:39:00.971
that you're just tripped up,

00:39:00.971 --> 00:39:04.007
family problems, money problems,

00:39:04.007 --> 00:39:05.308
you're doing the job
you don't wanna do,

00:39:05.308 --> 00:39:07.544
like the whole thing
seems like it's off.

00:39:07.544 --> 00:39:08.746
Do you have some tricks

00:39:08.746 --> 00:39:10.181
that can kind of just
get someone going?

00:39:10.181 --> 00:39:12.182 align:start size:84% position:16%
'Cause it seems
like that first step

00:39:12.182 --> 00:39:14.051 align:start size:97% position:3%
is often the biggest hurdle.
- Oh, sure.

00:39:14.051 --> 00:39:15.853
So I have a whole chapter,

00:39:15.853 --> 00:39:18.789
so it speaks partially to
the question you're asking,

00:39:18.789 --> 00:39:20.057
I have a whole chapter

00:39:20.057 --> 00:39:24.728
on sort of how can you
minimize regret in life?

00:39:24.728 --> 00:39:28.799 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because regret in a sense
is that existential,

00:39:28.799 --> 00:39:31.268 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, voice that
keeps coming in your head

00:39:31.268 --> 00:39:33.471
that makes you think, what
if, what if I had done this,

00:39:33.471 --> 00:39:35.105
what if I had done that, right?

00:39:35.105 --> 00:39:39.876
And so many of us think that
opportunities for happiness

00:39:39.876 --> 00:39:40.911
have passed us by.

00:39:40.911 --> 00:39:42.980
We're too old to be, let's say,

00:39:42.980 --> 00:39:44.281
I know that you love the NBA.

00:39:44.281 --> 00:39:45.816
Well, that is true.

00:39:45.816 --> 00:39:47.585
That is objectively true.

00:39:47.585 --> 00:39:50.521
It would be silly for you or I

00:39:50.521 --> 00:39:53.156
to think about being NBA stars,

00:39:53.156 --> 00:39:56.260
although you have seen some
of my three point shots,

00:39:56.260 --> 00:39:58.795
so maybe I can still make it.

00:39:58.795 --> 00:40:00.231 align:start size:97% position:3%
Plus, Muggsy Bogues was 5'2".
- We have had some good

00:40:00.231 --> 00:40:01.398
back and forth.

00:40:01.398 --> 00:40:02.633
- That's right. That's right.

00:40:02.633 --> 00:40:06.436
But no, but seriously,
there are many cases

00:40:06.436 --> 00:40:10.541 align:start size:97% position:3%
where that sense of, "I
can't get out of this funk,"

00:40:10.541 --> 00:40:14.478
is sort of a deterministic doom.

00:40:14.478 --> 00:40:15.679
Whereas in reality,

00:40:15.679 --> 00:40:17.881 align:start size:84% position:16%
you've placed this
restriction in your...

00:40:17.881 --> 00:40:20.117
So let me give you,
again, two examples.

00:40:21.318 --> 00:40:24.588 align:start size:84% position:16%
Example one, this
is from in the book.

00:40:24.588 --> 00:40:28.892
There's a gentleman who
left pre-Nazi Germany,

00:40:28.892 --> 00:40:30.193
he was a Jewish guy,

00:40:30.193 --> 00:40:32.029
left and came to Canada,

00:40:32.029 --> 00:40:33.864
had always wanted to study

00:40:33.864 --> 00:40:36.100
and, you know, get a
university education,

00:40:36.100 --> 00:40:38.936
but life circumstances
didn't allow him to.

00:40:38.936 --> 00:40:42.873
He comes to Canada as a
young kid, goes to work,

00:40:42.873 --> 00:40:47.244
has a successful career,
retires in his sixties.

00:40:47.244 --> 00:40:49.413
Now, he's in his sixties, right?

00:40:49.413 --> 00:40:52.550 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, average
undergrad is 20, 19.

00:40:52.550 --> 00:40:54.251
He says, "You know what?

00:40:54.251 --> 00:40:56.487
I'm of good mind, I have time.

00:40:56.487 --> 00:40:58.022
Why don't I sign up?"

00:40:58.022 --> 00:41:00.924 align:start size:94% position:6%
He actually went to the
university that I work at.

00:41:00.924 --> 00:41:02.158
And he said, "Why
don't I sign up

00:41:02.158 --> 00:41:03.960 align:start size:88% position:13%
and take some
undergraduate courses?"

00:41:03.960 --> 00:41:07.131 align:start size:88% position:13%
Lo and behold, a
few years later, he
finishes his undergrad.

00:41:07.131 --> 00:41:08.498
Then he says, "Hey,
I'm still young.

00:41:08.498 --> 00:41:09.733
I'm now in my seventies.

00:41:09.733 --> 00:41:12.269
Why don't I pursue
my master's degree?"

00:41:12.269 --> 00:41:13.637
Finishes it.

00:41:13.637 --> 00:41:16.773 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then in his
eighties, starts his PhD.

00:41:16.773 --> 00:41:19.075
And I remember,
this was in 1996,

00:41:19.075 --> 00:41:21.945
it was very early in
my academic career,

00:41:21.945 --> 00:41:25.149 align:start size:88% position:13%
the university
newspaper had the title,

00:41:25.149 --> 00:41:27.918 align:start size:88% position:13%
something like, "Finally
a Doctor at 91."

00:41:27.918 --> 00:41:31.922
And so he finishes his PhD and
within a year he passes away.

00:41:31.922 --> 00:41:33.857
So he could have easily said,

00:41:33.857 --> 00:41:36.460
"You know, I missed my
opportunity to study."

00:41:36.460 --> 00:41:39.429 align:start position:0%
Well, no, you had an opportunity
to do it and you did it.

00:41:39.429 --> 00:41:43.667 align:start size:97% position:3%
Second example, I just had
this guy last year on my show.

00:41:43.667 --> 00:41:46.370
This is a physician
who got, I think,

00:41:46.370 --> 00:41:50.374 align:start size:97% position:3%
his medical degree at
University of Vienna in 1955.

00:41:50.374 --> 00:41:53.577 align:start size:94% position:6%
Then while he was training
to be a hematologist

00:41:53.577 --> 00:41:58.015 align:start size:88% position:13%
as a specialist, picked
up a PhD in 1967.

00:41:58.015 --> 00:41:59.283
You weren't born yet.

00:41:59.283 --> 00:42:01.218 align:start size:88% position:13%
I was two years old, two
and a half years old.

00:42:01.218 --> 00:42:04.121 align:start size:88% position:13%
But he had always wanted
to be a physicist.

00:42:04.121 --> 00:42:06.657
His real love was physics.

00:42:06.657 --> 00:42:09.126
But his family had
told him, Jewish guy,

00:42:09.126 --> 00:42:10.394 align:start size:84% position:16%
"You have to do
something practical,

00:42:10.394 --> 00:42:12.363
become a physician,
blah, blah, blah."

00:42:12.363 --> 00:42:13.663
And he did it.

00:42:13.663 --> 00:42:16.567
At the age of 89,

00:42:16.567 --> 00:42:19.870 align:start size:84% position:16%
he completed a
second PhD in physics

00:42:19.870 --> 00:42:21.238
at Brown University.

00:42:21.238 --> 00:42:23.907 align:start size:88% position:13%
So why am I telling you,
again, these stories?

00:42:23.907 --> 00:42:26.710
Because oftentimes
we are in a funk

00:42:26.710 --> 00:42:29.346
because we place
these arbitrary,

00:42:29.346 --> 00:42:32.115 align:start size:84% position:16%
illusionary barriers
in our minds.

00:42:32.115 --> 00:42:34.217
Yes, we can't be
future NBA stars.

00:42:34.217 --> 00:42:35.652
We're too old for that.

00:42:35.652 --> 00:42:36.887
We can't be ballerinas,

00:42:36.887 --> 00:42:39.122
we're not gonna be
maestro violinists.

00:42:39.122 --> 00:42:41.791
But for many other things
that would make us happy,

00:42:41.791 --> 00:42:44.661 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's only our inner voice
that's stopping us from doing,

00:42:44.661 --> 00:42:46.597 align:start size:84% position:16%
so get off the
couch and go do it.

00:42:47.664 --> 00:42:49.466 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Can you be too
happy, do you think?

00:42:49.466 --> 00:42:50.868
I mean, we all know the person

00:42:50.868 --> 00:42:52.469 align:start size:84% position:16%
that just kinda
lives in the clouds

00:42:52.469 --> 00:42:53.671 align:start size:84% position:16%
that may be smiling
all the time,

00:42:53.671 --> 00:42:55.372
but really isn't on the ball

00:42:55.372 --> 00:42:57.975
or basically isn't
on planet earth.

00:42:59.009 --> 00:43:00.177
- Lex Fridman.

00:43:00.177 --> 00:43:01.911 align:start size:84% position:16%
Sorry, I had something
stuck in my...

00:43:01.911 --> 00:43:04.581 align:start position:0%
Well, he's not really, not that
I wanted to talk about him,

00:43:04.581 --> 00:43:08.318
but I argue he engages
in faux happiness, right?

00:43:08.318 --> 00:43:11.655
He's always, "I love you,
you love me, let's hug."

00:43:11.655 --> 00:43:12.889
I think that-

00:43:12.889 --> 00:43:14.391
- What was his tweet
a couple weeks ago

00:43:14.391 --> 00:43:15.693
that everybody went crazy on,

00:43:15.693 --> 00:43:17.060 align:start size:81% position:19%
I think you really
nailed him on?

00:43:17.060 --> 00:43:18.495
Wasn't there something

00:43:18.495 --> 00:43:20.297
not too long ago?
- "I don't care

00:43:20.297 --> 00:43:23.667 align:start size:88% position:13%
whether you call
yourself male or female,

00:43:23.667 --> 00:43:26.503
I care about what
your character is,"

00:43:26.503 --> 00:43:27.704
or something like that.

00:43:27.704 --> 00:43:29.239
And that pissed me off.

00:43:29.239 --> 00:43:31.641
Now again, people think
when I go after someone,

00:43:31.641 --> 00:43:33.043
I'm trying to be mean.

00:43:33.043 --> 00:43:35.345 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'm not, and that's because
there's a part of me

00:43:35.345 --> 00:43:37.147 align:start size:81% position:19%
that's a dogged
defender of truth.

00:43:37.147 --> 00:43:39.316
And actually, I talk
about this in the book,

00:43:39.316 --> 00:43:42.586
that existential authenticity

00:43:42.586 --> 00:43:45.422 align:start size:84% position:16%
is a sure pathway
to happiness, right?

00:43:46.756 --> 00:43:50.127
I present myself to the world
as though I'm 18 feet tall

00:43:50.127 --> 00:43:52.128
because I'm always authentic.

00:43:52.128 --> 00:43:55.165 align:start size:97% position:3%
I really know my qualities,
I know my faults.

00:43:55.165 --> 00:43:57.034
I don't modulate anything.

00:43:57.034 --> 00:43:59.737 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so there are no
fissures in my personhood,

00:43:59.737 --> 00:44:02.906 align:start size:97% position:3%
and that's why I then can feel
comfortable in any setting

00:44:02.906 --> 00:44:06.243 align:start size:84% position:16%
because I don't
have to retain lies.

00:44:06.243 --> 00:44:08.411
For better or worse, here I am.

00:44:08.411 --> 00:44:10.981
And what I was pissed
with Lex is, you know,

00:44:12.115 --> 00:44:13.617
I didn't know who
Lex was, frankly,

00:44:13.617 --> 00:44:15.752
but then people said, "Oh,
you should go on his show."

00:44:15.752 --> 00:44:17.488
That's how I started
getting to know him.

00:44:17.488 --> 00:44:20.257
And then I would see
all this inane bullshit

00:44:20.257 --> 00:44:23.327 align:start position:0%
that he would consistently
post, and it just pissed me off.

00:44:23.327 --> 00:44:26.497
So in his case, I don't know
if he's being real or not.

00:44:26.497 --> 00:44:30.234
He has gone into
the extra happiness

00:44:30.234 --> 00:44:31.735
because not everything in life

00:44:31.735 --> 00:44:33.937 align:start size:84% position:16%
is about hugging and
happiness, right?

00:44:33.937 --> 00:44:37.207
I mean, that's a false narrative

00:44:37.207 --> 00:44:38.942 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that's why I
tried to go after him.

00:44:38.942 --> 00:44:43.013
And now to his lack of
credit, he always says,

00:44:43.013 --> 00:44:45.348 align:start size:88% position:13%
"Oh, I always am willing
to learn from anyone

00:44:45.348 --> 00:44:46.550
and speak to anyone."

00:44:46.550 --> 00:44:48.852
First thing he does
is he blocks me.

00:44:48.852 --> 00:44:50.120
What a schmuck.

00:44:50.120 --> 00:44:51.554
(Dave laughs)

00:44:51.554 --> 00:44:54.023 align:start position:0%
- So you can be happy and still
think someone's a schmuck.

00:44:54.023 --> 00:44:55.325
That's good, that
should definitely-

00:44:55.325 --> 00:44:56.960
- Exactly right.

00:44:56.960 --> 00:45:00.130 align:start size:97% position:3%
And by the way, to that
point, people often say to me,

00:45:00.130 --> 00:45:01.631
when they meet me,

00:45:01.631 --> 00:45:04.668
"Oh, you seem so much
nicer and friendlier

00:45:04.668 --> 00:45:06.102
than on social media."

00:45:06.102 --> 00:45:10.541 align:start position:0%
No, because this is called
situational specificity, right?

00:45:10.541 --> 00:45:14.011
If you attack me in an alley,

00:45:14.011 --> 00:45:16.680
I might be violent 'cause
you're trying to mug me.

00:45:16.680 --> 00:45:19.416
That's not because I'm
dispositionally violent,

00:45:19.416 --> 00:45:21.184
it's because I act differently

00:45:21.184 --> 00:45:23.019
when I'm tucking
my children to bed

00:45:23.019 --> 00:45:25.389
and when I'm being
mugged in an alley.

00:45:25.389 --> 00:45:29.026 align:start size:97% position:3%
So when I am being mugged
by assholes on social media,

00:45:29.026 --> 00:45:30.827
I act in a spicier manner

00:45:30.827 --> 00:45:33.664
not because I'm not
happy or I'm mean,

00:45:33.664 --> 00:45:35.098 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's because
different strategies

00:45:35.098 --> 00:45:36.934
require different approaches.

00:45:36.934 --> 00:45:38.469
I mean, or different context.

00:45:39.269 --> 00:45:40.737
- So you're a little happier

00:45:40.737 --> 00:45:43.473 align:start size:94% position:6%
when you're basically
putting your kids to sleep

00:45:43.473 --> 00:45:44.841 align:start size:84% position:16%
than you are when
you're being mugged.

00:45:44.841 --> 00:45:46.576
I just wanna make sure
I got that one clear.

00:45:46.576 --> 00:45:50.280
- That is a correct
statement, indeed.

00:45:50.280 --> 00:45:51.948
- What about the
things that people

00:45:51.948 --> 00:45:55.018 align:start size:88% position:13%
at least get the
temporary happiness on,

00:45:55.018 --> 00:45:57.855
whether it might be the booze

00:45:57.855 --> 00:45:59.956
or the extra indulgence in food

00:45:59.956 --> 00:46:04.027
or whatever they might be
smoking or the other stuff?

00:46:04.027 --> 00:46:05.128
- Completely fleeting.

00:46:05.128 --> 00:46:06.963
So I talk about in the book,

00:46:06.963 --> 00:46:10.066 align:start size:97% position:3%
as someone who studies
consumer psychology, right,

00:46:10.066 --> 00:46:13.036
so I wanted to link it to
some of my own interests.

00:46:13.036 --> 00:46:15.205 align:start size:84% position:16%
So there's been
now several studies

00:46:15.205 --> 00:46:18.875 align:start size:84% position:16%
that have looked at
whether spending money

00:46:18.875 --> 00:46:20.810
on material possessions,

00:46:20.810 --> 00:46:22.479
so that speaks to kind
of your point, right?

00:46:22.479 --> 00:46:23.981
Should I get the next Prada bag

00:46:23.981 --> 00:46:26.517 align:start size:84% position:16%
or the really cool
Gucci or the Maserati?

00:46:27.651 --> 00:46:30.821
What brings you greater
long-term happiness,

00:46:30.821 --> 00:46:34.324
the spending money on
material possessions

00:46:34.324 --> 00:46:36.326
or on experiences?

00:46:36.326 --> 00:46:40.530 align:start size:97% position:3%
And overwhelmingly,
repeatedly, the research shows

00:46:40.530 --> 00:46:43.633
that for long-term
enduring happiness,

00:46:43.633 --> 00:46:46.269
living a life of
rich experiences

00:46:46.269 --> 00:46:49.139 align:start size:94% position:6%
is going to bring you a
lot more bang for the buck

00:46:49.139 --> 00:46:50.773
than if you spend
it on a Ferrari.

00:46:50.773 --> 00:46:52.643
And I know this from
personal experience

00:46:52.643 --> 00:46:54.544
because one of my brothers

00:46:54.544 --> 00:46:57.146
who until recently lived
in Southern California

00:46:57.146 --> 00:47:00.450 align:start size:94% position:6%
and was very, very wealthy
during the dotcom era,

00:47:00.450 --> 00:47:03.320 align:start size:88% position:13%
he had not one, not
two, but three Ferraris.

00:47:03.320 --> 00:47:05.055
He had an Aston Martin Lagonda.

00:47:05.055 --> 00:47:09.292 align:start size:97% position:3%
He was a collector of
conspicuous consumption goods.

00:47:09.292 --> 00:47:13.163 align:start size:97% position:3%
I hate to say it, and I
don't say this with any glee,

00:47:14.297 --> 00:47:16.600
I don't think today
he's happy, right?

00:47:16.600 --> 00:47:19.036
Whereas I never drove a Ferrari,

00:47:19.036 --> 00:47:20.804
I never had access to a Ferrari,

00:47:20.804 --> 00:47:23.739 align:start size:94% position:6%
but later today, my
children are going to come

00:47:23.739 --> 00:47:26.309 align:start position:0%
and are gonna say, "Oh, how
was your chat with Dave Rubin?"

00:47:26.309 --> 00:47:30.346 align:start size:97% position:3%
That moment right there
probably makes me wealthier

00:47:30.346 --> 00:47:32.316 align:start size:84% position:16%
than all of his
Ferraris put together.

00:47:33.417 --> 00:47:35.652
- When you get to
the happiness part,

00:47:35.652 --> 00:47:37.454
so the place that you're
basically at in your life

00:47:37.454 --> 00:47:38.922
at this moment,

00:47:40.523 --> 00:47:42.525 align:start size:94% position:6%
do you have to keep
doing work to sustain it?

00:47:42.525 --> 00:47:44.494
Or at that point, if you
feel like you got it right,

00:47:44.494 --> 00:47:46.028
you can just kind
of ride it out?

00:47:46.028 --> 00:47:48.332 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, do you feel like there
are things on the horizon

00:47:48.332 --> 00:47:50.100
beyond you have to
keep doing your work,

00:47:50.100 --> 00:47:51.668
keep maintaining that
good relationship?

00:47:51.668 --> 00:47:53.270
Is there anything
else you gotta do

00:47:53.270 --> 00:47:55.105
to really keep pushing forward?

00:47:55.105 --> 00:47:56.739 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, I think, yeah,
amazing question.

00:47:56.739 --> 00:48:01.111 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think you have to keep
implementing those strategies

00:48:01.111 --> 00:48:03.947
that ensure that you are
going to remain happy.

00:48:03.947 --> 00:48:05.649
If I were to analogize it,

00:48:05.649 --> 00:48:10.721
if I now get off my very
obsessive weight concerns,

00:48:13.022 --> 00:48:14.224
it won't take me long

00:48:14.224 --> 00:48:16.693 align:start size:88% position:13%
before we get two
times Gad again, right?

00:48:16.693 --> 00:48:20.130
In other words, I mean,
literally, I probably-

00:48:20.130 --> 00:48:22.432 align:start size:88% position:13%
- You know, I have to
say, I have to say, Gad,

00:48:22.432 --> 00:48:24.567
two times weight
Gad, pretty happy.

00:48:24.567 --> 00:48:25.835
He was pretty happy

00:48:25.835 --> 00:48:27.237
as far as I can tell.
- I was pretty happy.

00:48:27.237 --> 00:48:30.540 align:start size:94% position:6%
Although my blood
pressure was not as happy,

00:48:30.540 --> 00:48:33.109 align:start size:94% position:6%
although my physician
was not as happy with me.

00:48:33.109 --> 00:48:35.078 align:start size:84% position:16%
So again, I think
from the perspective

00:48:35.078 --> 00:48:38.081 align:start size:94% position:6%
of extending my happiness
as far as I can go,

00:48:38.081 --> 00:48:40.750
literally in terms
of life expectancy

00:48:40.750 --> 00:48:44.454 align:start size:97% position:3%
and contrary to what
Professor Dr. Lizzo tells us,

00:48:45.989 --> 00:48:47.357
you know what I'm talking about,

00:48:47.357 --> 00:48:50.027 align:start size:88% position:13%
right, Lizzo's got-
- Yes, I got it, Lizzo.

00:48:50.027 --> 00:48:51.928
They're fortifying the stage.

00:48:51.928 --> 00:48:53.263
- They're fortifying the stage.

00:48:53.263 --> 00:48:54.898
And she argues that, you know,

00:48:54.898 --> 00:48:56.566 align:start size:84% position:16%
"Stop talking
about this nonsense

00:48:56.566 --> 00:48:59.302 align:start size:88% position:13%
about health being
related to your weight.

00:48:59.302 --> 00:49:00.971
That's just a construct."

00:49:02.272 --> 00:49:04.474 align:start size:84% position:16%
My life expectancy,
knock on wood,

00:49:04.474 --> 00:49:06.476
is hopefully going to, you know,

00:49:06.476 --> 00:49:09.512
drastically increase
by virtue of, you know,

00:49:09.512 --> 00:49:12.415
my having now be at
the right weight.

00:49:12.415 --> 00:49:15.585
But I have to always
be careful, right?

00:49:15.585 --> 00:49:18.154 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so to your point
about happiness,

00:49:18.154 --> 00:49:21.190
I don't think you can get to
Mount Happiness, to the summit,

00:49:21.190 --> 00:49:24.594
and say, "I've done it all,
now let me take crystal meth

00:49:24.594 --> 00:49:26.362 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I will continue
to be happy."

00:49:26.362 --> 00:49:28.398
You still have to work
on your relationships,

00:49:28.398 --> 00:49:29.665
you still have to be playful,

00:49:29.665 --> 00:49:31.567
you still have to
nourish your mind.

00:49:31.567 --> 00:49:34.570
All those things are
perpetual pursuits.

00:49:34.570 --> 00:49:37.040 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Gad, watch very closely
the screen right now,

00:49:37.040 --> 00:49:40.844
because I'm gonna wink at you
as I ask you this question.

00:49:40.844 --> 00:49:44.281
Can you mindfully meditate
yourself into happiness?

00:49:44.281 --> 00:49:47.451
(Gad laughs)

00:49:47.451 --> 00:49:48.685 align:start size:84% position:16%
- It's incredible.
- Some of the audience

00:49:48.685 --> 00:49:51.120 align:start size:88% position:13%
will know what I just
asked and some will not.

00:49:51.120 --> 00:49:52.755 align:start size:75% position:25%
It's okay.
- Yes, yes, yes.

00:49:52.755 --> 00:49:55.124
As a matter of fact, so
in one of the chapters,

00:49:55.124 --> 00:49:57.961 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'm gonna come to your mindful
meditation in a second,

00:49:57.961 --> 00:50:01.598 align:start size:84% position:16%
in one of the
chapters I talk about

00:50:01.598 --> 00:50:05.568
the fundamentally most
powerful law in nature,

00:50:05.568 --> 00:50:08.304
which is what I call
the inverted U curve.

00:50:08.304 --> 00:50:12.275
Inverted U, which the ancient
Greeks already knew about,

00:50:12.275 --> 00:50:14.944 align:start size:84% position:16%
is too little of
something is not good,

00:50:14.944 --> 00:50:16.646 align:start size:84% position:16%
too much of something
is not good,

00:50:16.646 --> 00:50:18.982 align:start size:88% position:13%
and the sweet spot is
somewhere in the middle,

00:50:18.982 --> 00:50:21.918
which Aristotle referred
to as the golden mean,

00:50:21.918 --> 00:50:24.354
which Buddha referred
to as the middle way.

00:50:24.354 --> 00:50:25.955
Maimonides referred to it.

00:50:25.955 --> 00:50:27.790
And so in that chapter,

00:50:27.790 --> 00:50:32.863 align:start position:0%
I demonstrate that across a
bewildering number of contexts,

00:50:33.530 --> 00:50:35.198
that inverted U applies,

00:50:35.198 --> 00:50:38.502
including to mindful meditation,

00:50:38.502 --> 00:50:41.772
meaning that if you
never are mindful

00:50:41.772 --> 00:50:44.374 align:start position:0%
in terms of meditating or taking
a deep breath or something,

00:50:44.374 --> 00:50:45.609
it's not good,

00:50:45.609 --> 00:50:47.377 align:start size:84% position:16%
but doing it too
much, it's not good.

00:50:47.377 --> 00:50:50.013
And actually that
speaks to a tweet

00:50:50.013 --> 00:50:53.116
that I think Elon Musk.
- I think Elon Musk sent-

00:50:53.116 --> 00:50:55.852 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Had sent to that person
in question saying,

00:50:55.852 --> 00:50:59.456 align:start size:97% position:3%
"So and so, there is such a
thing as too much meditation."

00:50:59.456 --> 00:51:00.790 align:start size:84% position:16%
So that exactly
speaks to your point.

00:51:00.790 --> 00:51:03.760
Always find the middle
way, the sweet spot.

00:51:03.760 --> 00:51:05.328
You'll be happier.

00:51:05.328 --> 00:51:08.365
- Gad, I have to say one
of my great joys in life

00:51:08.365 --> 00:51:11.468 align:start size:97% position:3%
is that I think I found the
spouse I was supposed to find.

00:51:11.468 --> 00:51:12.535
